(*
porta space-sync (tuvm#108) — a reply is matched to the LEG THAT ASKED FOR IT.

The bridge's two legs share one socket: the exchange sends a #pull and waits in
`pulling`, then sends a #push and waits in `pushing` (rete#440 R2: the waits are
states, moved on by the socket's doorbell). Answering the first datagram to arrive,
whatever it was, once handed a #batch delayed past the pull's budget to `applyAck:`,
which rejected it (moving nothing) and left the real #ack unread. Microseconds of
exposure on loopback; milliseconds over a mesh, which is why plan 3 could not have
caught it.

The rig below makes that window certain rather than likely: each leg is fed the
OTHER leg's reply first, then its own. The socket is a doorbell-driven fake: a ring
is a post on its channel, and the bridge reads it to EMPTY (design invariant 4). The
first ring finds the stray #ack and the real #batch, then an empty answer; the
second finds the stray #batch and the real #ack.

What is asserted, and why each one is falsifiable:

- `acked untouched by cross 0` — the first ring moved the pull leg on and handed
  the push leg nothing yet, so the crossed #ack (hw = 99) is proven not to have
  moved the watermark on its way past.
- `cursor 7` — the pull leg walked PAST a crossed #ack and found its #batch. It is
  also the assertion that the crossed #batch handed to the push leg (hi = 99) was
  DISCARDED and not applied: if a skipped datagram were applied on the way past,
  this reads 99.
- `acked applied true` — the push leg walked past a crossed #batch and found its
  #ack.
- `crossed 2` — the skips are COUNTED, not silent. An absence proves nothing; a
  count is what distinguishes "the window never opened" from "it opened twice and
  the bridge handled it".
- `misses 0` — skipping must not cost a leg its reply.

Discarding rather than applying a crossed reply is safe for exactly the reason the
protocol has no retransmit layer: the exchange is idempotent by cursor in both
directions. A dropped #batch is re-sent on the next pull (the cursor never moved);
a dropped #ack costs a re-push that porta dedupes by seq.

An #err is deliberately NOT skipped — it is a real terminal answer to whichever
leg asked, and waiting out the full budget for a reply porta will never send would
turn a one-datagram rejection into a 2-second stall plus a miss. Both #errs arrive
under ONE ring, so the second one is read in the push leg the first one opened.
expect:
pushing after the first ring true
acked untouched by cross 0
cursor 7
acked applied true
crossed 2
misses 0
porta refused this node: malformed
err is not skipped true
err counted as a refusal 1
err is not a miss 0
err leg left idle true
*)
class ReplyTagMatch packageUsing: manifest = () (

  (* Duck-typed stand-ins: PortaBridge only ever sends `sendTo:port:data:`,
     `receive` and `doorbell` to its socket, and `data` to what receive answers. *)
  public class FakeDatagram = ( | private data | ) (
    public setData: d = ( data:: d. ^ self )
    public data = ( ^ data )
  ) : (
    public on: d = ( ^ self new setData: d )
  )

  (* A five-slot scripted socket. Slots are stepped one per `receive`; a nil slot
     (and everything past the fifth) answers nil, which is what an emptied socket
     looks like — the answer that ends a read. Five explicit slots rather than a
     collection: the kernel instance an app is handed vends no OrderedCollection. *)
  public class FakeSocket = ( | private bell private a private b private c private d private e private n | ) (
    public setBell: p a: v b: w c: x d: y e: z = ( bell:: p. a:: v. b:: w. c:: x. d:: y. e:: z. n:: 0. ^ self )
    public doorbell = ( ^ bell )
    public sendTo: host port: p data: bytes = ( ^ self )
    public receive = ( | slot |
      n:: n + 1.
      slot:: nil.
      n = 1 ifTrue: [ slot:: a ].
      n = 2 ifTrue: [ slot:: b ].
      n = 3 ifTrue: [ slot:: c ].
      n = 4 ifTrue: [ slot:: d ].
      n = 5 ifTrue: [ slot:: e ].
      slot == nil ifTrue: [ ^ nil ].
      ^ FakeDatagram on: slot )
  ) : (
    public bell: p a: v b: w c: x d: y e: z = ( ^ self new setBell: p a: v b: w c: x d: y e: z )
  )

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bridge hw strayAck realBatch strayBatch realAck sock err errBridge errSock spans timers |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    timers:: platform timers.
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :t | timers dispatch: t ].

    (* Something for the push leg to send, so `pushFrame` is not nil and the
       second wait actually happens. *)
    space put: 'obs/health' value: 11.
    hw:: space seq.

    (* The crossed pair. hw = 99 and hi = 99 are far above any real seq here, so
       either one leaking into the wrong applier is loud rather than subtle. *)
    strayAck:: platform Cbor encode:
      (platform Record tag: #ack with: #hw -> 99).
    realBatch:: platform Cbor encode:
      (platform Record tag: #batch
         with: #hi -> 7
         with: #ent -> (platform List with:
            (platform List with: 7 with: 'goal/name' with: 2 with: 'bench'))).
    strayBatch:: platform Cbor encode:
      (platform Record tag: #batch
         with: #hi -> 99
         with: #ent -> (platform List with:
            (platform List with: 99 with: 'goal/name' with: 2 with: 'leaked'))).
    realAck:: platform Cbor encode:
      (platform Record tag: #ack with: #hw -> hw).

    sock:: FakeSocket bell: 'test/net/a' a: strayAck b: realBatch c: nil d: strayBatch e: realAck.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: sock space: space id: 'n1' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans timers: timers.

    bridge checkIn.
    space out: 'test/net/a' value: 1.
    space drain.
    ('pushing after the first ring ' , (bridge state = #pushing) printString) out.
    ('acked untouched by cross ' , bridge acked printString) out.
    space out: 'test/net/a' value: 1.
    space drain.
    ('cursor ' , bridge cursor printString) out.
    ('acked applied ' , (bridge acked = hw) printString) out.
    ('crossed ' , bridge crossed printString) out.
    ('misses ' , bridge misses printString) out.

    (* An #err answers the leg. It must come straight back — not be skipped as
       foreign traffic — so the leg fails fast instead of burning its budget. *)
    err:: platform Cbor encode: (platform Record tag: #err with: #msg -> 'malformed').
    errSock:: FakeSocket bell: 'test/net/b' a: err b: err c: nil d: nil e: nil.
    errBridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: errSock space: space id: 'n2' peer: '::1' port: 6969 spans: spans timers: timers.
    errBridge checkIn.
    space out: 'test/net/b' value: 1.
    space drain.
    ('err is not skipped ' , (errBridge crossed = 0) printString) out.

    (* rete#199 — and it is COUNTED, which is the half that was missing. An #err
       incremented neither `misses` nor `acked`, so a node being uniformly refused
       reported the same numbers as a healthy one: `misses 0` all the way to the
       arena death that made this a two-hour diagnosis. The message above prints
       ONCE per reason, not once per tick.

       ONE per check-in, not one per datagram: both legs are refused here, and only
       the push leg's `applyAck:` counts. That keeps `ref` directly comparable with
       `wak` and `pto`, which are also per-check-in. *)
    ('err counted as a refusal ' , errBridge refused printString) out.
    ('err is not a miss ' , errBridge misses printString) out.
    ('err leg left idle ' , (errBridge state = #idle) printString) out.
    ^ 0
  )
) : (
  public packageUsing: manifest = ( ^ self new )
)
